# Shell scripts MUST be LF in the index and on disk. They run on ubuntu in
# GitHub Actions and on the Linux boxes; a CRLF shebang line dies there with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" — a failure that names nothing relevant.
# Not hypothetical: PowerShell's bare `bash` is WSL, whose git does not read
# the Windows global core.autocrlf, so a commit from that side puts CRLF in the
# index. Pinned here so correctness does not depend on every  client being configured.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts are Windows-only and pinned to CRLF for the same reason the
# .sh rule exists: so correctness does not depend on any client's autocrlf. Note
# the endings are NOT the encoding hazard here -- install.ps1 must also stay pure
# ASCII, because Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a BOM-less file as ANSI and a UTF-8
# em-dash then decodes to a string-terminating quote. Enforced by
# TestWindowsInstallScriptIsAscii.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
